                %li
                  %a{:href => "#geometric_quantization"} Geometric quantization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#categorification"} Categorification
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p “Harmonic oscillator” is a fancy name for a rock on a spring:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
                it is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
                given by a point mass in a parabolic potential, feeling
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forces"} forces
                driving it back to a specified origin that are propertional to the distance of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
                from that origin.
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                and in particular
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                the quantum harmonic oscillator governs not just the dynamics of idealized point masses but crucially appears in the dynamics of all free massive quantum fields.
          %p To quote the field theorist Sidney Coleman,
          %blockquote
            %p The career of a young theoretical physicist consists of treating the harmonic oscillator in ever-increasing levels of abstraction.
          %h2#classical_oscillator Classical oscillator
          %p
            First the harmonic oscillator in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            exerted by a spring is proportional to how far you stretch it:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi k
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = k x.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potential+energy"} potential energy
            stored in a stretched spring is the integral of that:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_0 = \frac{1}{2}k x^2 + C,
          %p
            and to make things work out nicely, we’re going to choose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = -1/2.
            The total
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
            is the sum of the potential and the kinetic energy:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mi T
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi m
                  %msup
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0 = V_0 + T = \frac{1}{2}k x^2 + \frac{1}{2}m v^2 - \frac{1}{2}.
          %p
            By choosing units so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi m
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = m = 1,
            we get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0 = \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{p^2}{2} - \frac{1}{2},
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is momentum.
          %h2#quantum_harmonic_oscillator Quantum harmonic oscillator
          %p
            Now the harmonic oscillator in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            We
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantize
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi i
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi x
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = -i \frac{\partial}{\partial x},
            taking units where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar = 1.
            Now
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi n
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mi p
                          %mo −
                          %mi p
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi n
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                          %mfrac
                            %mo ∂
                            %mrow
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi x
                          %mo +
                          %mi i
                          %mfrac
                            %mo ∂
                            %mrow
                              %mo ∂
                              %mi x
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi n
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mi i
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi n
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ [x, p]x^n &amp; = &amp; (x p - p x)x^n \\ &amp; = &amp;(- x i \frac{\partial}{\partial x} + i \frac{\partial}{\partial x} x)x^n \\ &amp; = &amp; -i(n x^n - (n+1)x^n) \\ &amp; = &amp; i x^n. }
          %p
            If we define a new
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi ix
                    %msqrt
                      %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = \frac{p + ix}{\sqrt{2}},
            then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi z
                          %msup
                            %mi z
                            %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi p
                              %mo +
                              %mi i
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msqrt
                              %mn 2
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi p
                              %mo −
                              %mi i
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msqrt
                              %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac
                            %mn 1
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi p
                            %mn 2
                          %mo +
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mi p
                          %mo −
                          %mi p
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo +
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac
                            %mn 1
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi p
                            %mn 2
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                          %mo +
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mn 0
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ z z^* &amp; = &amp; \frac{(p + i x)}{\sqrt{2}} \frac{(p - i x)}{\sqrt{2}} \\ &amp; = &amp; \frac{1}{2}(p^2 + i(x p - p x) + x^2) \\ &amp; = &amp; \frac{1}{2}(p^2 -1 + x^2) \\ &amp; = &amp; H_0. }
          %p
            We can think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z^*
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dz
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d}{dz}
            and write the energy
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvector"} eigenvector
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo :
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi z
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dz
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0 z^n = z \frac{d}{dz} z^n = n z^n.
          %p
            The creation operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            adds a photon to the mix; there’s only one way to do that, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\cdot z^n = 1 z^{n+1}.
            The annihilation operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dz
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d}{dz}
            destroys one of the photons; in the state
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon"} photon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dz
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d}{dz} z^n = n z^{n-1}.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger%27s+equation"} Schrödinger's equation
            says
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dt
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \frac{d}{dt} \psi = H_0 \psi,
            so
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi itn
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi(t) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} e^{-itn} a_n z^n.
          %p
            This way of representing the state of a QHO is known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %em Fock basis
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hydrogen+atom"} hydrogen atom
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+lattice+system"} quantum lattice system
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Howard+Georgi"}> Howard Georgi
              , Section 1 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Physics of Waves
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.people.fas.harvard.edu/~hgeorgi/new.htm"} web
              %a{:href => "https://www.people.fas.harvard.edu/~hgeorgi/onenew.pdf"} pdf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %h3#geometric_quantization Geometric quantization
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of the harmonic oscillator is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Adrian Lim,
                %em A non-standard geometric quantization of the harmonic oscillator
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.cornell.edu/~pclim/Docs/papers/oscillator6.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                G. Sherry,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric quantization of the 3-dimensional harmonic oscillator
            %li
              %p
                Sergey V. Zuev,
                %em Geometric quantization of generalized oscillator
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9902024"}> arXiv:math-ph/9902024
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+D.+Popov"}> Alexander D. Popov
                ,
                %em Quantum charges of harmonic oscillators
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.01756"}> arXiv:2404.01756
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#categorification Categorification
          %p
            A program initiated by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
            aims to identify a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of sorts of the quantum harmonic oscillator
          %p The notes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ‘s
              %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/qg-fall2003/"} Fall ‘03
              Quantum gravity seminar
          %p
            relate wavefunctions expressed in the Fock basis to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+types"} structure types
          %p This originates in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Dolan"} James Dolan
              %em From finite sets to Feynman diagrams
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0004133"}> arXiv
              ).
          %p For more along these lines see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeffrey+Morton"}> Jeffrey Morton
              ,
              %em Categorified Algebra and Quantum Mechanics
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0601458"}> arXiv
              )
